  "interpretation": "The linear ETL chain has one node per layer, so layered parallelism cannot help it -- it is the control, and its speedup should be ~1.0x. The branched DAG puts quality/PII/chunking in one layer, which is where concurrency pays off; its ceiling is the layer width (3). With synthetic_latency_s = 0 and the offline lexicon backend the whole pipeline runs in milliseconds, so neither shape shows a difference: the scheduling win scales with per-node latency, which in production is dominated by LLM calls. Operators are IO-bound, which is why threads are the right primitive despite the GIL."
